Design choices made and their trade-offs
Multi-subscription Landing zone setup to simulate an ideal enterprise Azure environment
Trade-off: Added complexity to the initial setup
Management group hierarchy to provide centralized policy inheritance and governance across subscriptions.
Trade-off: 
Hub-and-Spoke Networking - A shared hub Virtual Network (VNet) in the Connectivity subscription provides the foundation for future workload spokes.
Trade-off: 
Cost-Controlled Design - Expensive services such as Azure Firewall, Virtual Private Network (VPN) Gateway, Bastion, DDoS Network Protection, Private DNS Resolver, and paid Microsoft Defender for Cloud plans were disabled for the lab.
Trade-off: 
Infrastructure as Code - Terraform manages the platform, while Azure DevOps provides Pull Request validation, planning, approvals, and deployment.
Trade-off: 
Monitoring - The Management subscription provides centralized monitoring using Log Analytics, Azure Monitor Data Collection Rules, managed identity, and Service Health alerts.
Trade-off: 
Security and Governance - Azure Policy and management-group inheritance provide centralized governance. The platform can be extended with Microsoft Defender for Cloud, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), diagnostic settings, and centralized security monitoring.
